1 DUMP

   Shows a file on a mounted volume in hexadecimal and as text, as
   VMS's DUMP does: a block at a time, or with /RECORD a record at a
   time.

   Format

     DUMP filespec

2 Parameter

 filespec

   One file on a mounted volume, with no wildcards.  Without a version,
   the newest.

2 Description

   Each line shows 32 bytes: eight longwords in hexadecimal, the highest
   first, so that each reads as a number; then the bytes as text, lowest
   first, with "." for a byte that can't be shown; then the offset of the
   line's first byte.

   By blocks (the default), DUMP shows each virtual block up to the
   file's end-of-file block.  By records, it shows each record with its
   record's file address (RFA), and a VFC record without its fixed
   control.

2 Qualifiers
/BLOCKS

      /BLOCKS[=(option[,...])]

   Shows the file's blocks, numbered from 1.  The options say which:

     START:n    The first block to show (1).
     END:n      The last block to show.
     COUNT:n    How many blocks to show.

   START=n, END=n, and COUNT=n are taken too.  This is the default.

/RECORD

      /RECORD[=(option[,...])]

   Shows the file's records, numbered from 1.  The options are /BLOCKS'
   options, counting records.

2 Examples

   1.$ DUMP/BLOCKS=COUNT:1 LOGIN.COM

     Shows LOGIN.COM's first block.

   2.$ DUMP/RECORD=(START:10,COUNT:5) DATA.DAT

     Shows records 10 to 14 of DATA.DAT.
